# Ghostty — the terminal.
#
# Every key below was checked against `ghostty +show-config --default` on 1.3.1
# before being written, and every keybind action against `ghostty +list-actions`.
# That is not pedantry: Ghostty silently ignores options it does not recognise,
# so an unchecked config looks carefully tuned and does nothing. Two keys were
# dropped during this pass for exactly that reason (`bold-is-bright` and
# `selection-invert-fg-bg` do not exist in 1.3.1).
#
# Verifying a change — and the limits of the obvious check:
#
#   ghostty +show-config | grep <key>
#
# A key ABSENT from that output means one of two things, indistinguishably:
# it matches Ghostty's default, or it was invalid and silently dropped. Tested:
# `scrollbar = __bogus__` produces no stderr and simply omits the key. So
# +show-config proves a value PARSED, never that it is correct or that it
# resolved. Two things it will not catch:
#   - a theme name that does not resolve (fails at load, shows a startup
#     "Configuration Errors" dialog — check `background`/`foreground` are set)
#   - a shader that does not compile
#
# CORRECTION, 2026-09-04. This block used to say a broken shader produced "the
# same dialog", and that the launch log printed `info(shadertoy): loaded custom
# shader` on success. BOTH ARE FALSE. Ghostty's own documentation for
# `custom-shader`:
#
#   "If the shader fails to compile, the shader will be ignored. Any errors
#    related to shader compilation will not show up as configuration errors
#    and only show up in the log, since shader compilation happens after
#    configuration loading on the dedicated render thread."
#
# And that log string does not exist anywhere in the 1.3.1 binary — checked
# with `strings`. So a broken shader is SILENTLY DROPPED: the terminal opens
# perfectly, +show-config still echoes the path back, and the effect is just
# gone. There is no green light to look for.
#
# This is why the compile harness below is not optional pedantry — with two
# shaders chained (there were briefly three; see the note above the
# `custom-shader` lines), it is the only thing standing between a typo and an
# effect that quietly stops existing. Two real bugs have already been caught
# this way: a local variable named `length` shadowing the GLSL builtin, and
# `iCursorVisible.x` on a uniform that is an int.

# ── font ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CORRECTION, 2026-09-04. This said the family string was "Cascadia Code NF",
# "not Cascadia Code Nerd Font". Both are wrong on this machine, and the note
# even described the exact symptom without noticing it was live:
#
#     fc-match 'Cascadia Code NF'   ->  AdwaitaSans-Regular.ttf
#
# The terminal was running on a PROPORTIONAL SANS. fontconfig substitutes rather
# than failing, Ghostty reports no error, and the config looks perfect.
#
# The cause is that the Arch PACKAGE is named after Cascadia Code but the FONTS
# in it are Nerd Fonts' patch of it, which renames the family to Caskaydia Cove:
#
#     pacman -Ql ttf-cascadia-code-nerd | grep TTF
#         /usr/share/fonts/TTF/CaskaydiaCoveNerdFont-Regular.ttf   (and 53 more)
#     fc-list : family | tr ',' '\n' | grep -i caskaydia | sort -u
#         CaskaydiaCove Nerd Font        <- this one
#         CaskaydiaCove Nerd Font Mono   <- icons squeezed into a single cell
#         CaskaydiaCove Nerd Font Propo  <- proportional; never for a terminal
#         CaskaydiaCove NF / NFM / NFP   <- abbreviated aliases of the same files
#
# Non-Mono on purpose: Ghostty handles a glyph wider than one cell correctly,
# and Starship's powerline separators want their design width. The abbreviated
# "NF" aliases also register every weight as a separate family
# ("CaskaydiaCove NF SemiLight"), which makes bold/italic selection worse - so
# the full name is the one to use.
#
# THE CHECK, and it is not `ghostty +show-config` (that echoes back any string
# that parsed, including a family that does not exist):
#     fc-match 'CaskaydiaCove Nerd Font'   # must name a CaskaydiaCove*.ttf
# scripts/doctor.sh now runs exactly that, because this failed silently for
# weeks.
font-family = CaskaydiaCove Nerd Font
{{ if eq .profile "laptop" -}}
# 10 at ~157 ppi with Plasma at 125%. See the desktop branch for why this is
# not one number.
font-size = 10
{{- else -}}
# 12 at ~102 ppi with Plasma at 100%. NB: a point is supposed to be a physical
# unit, so "the same font-size looks the same everywhere" - it does not, because
# Plasma's scale factor multiplies it and the two machines run different
# factors. 10 x 1.25 on the laptop and 12 x 1.0 here land in the same place.
# If you change the desktop's Plasma scale, change this with it.
#
# NB: on the desktop this line does NOT appear in `ghostty +show-config`, and
# that is fine - 12 is Ghostty's own default (`ghostty +show-config --default`
# confirms it), and the header above explains that show-config omits anything
# matching a default. So the desktop hits the one case where "absent" is
# indistinguishable from "silently rejected". Verified the honest way instead:
# setting 13 makes the key appear, setting __bogus__ makes it vanish - so the
# file IS parsed and 12 IS accepted. Do not "fix" its absence.
font-size = 12
{{- end }}

# NB: only two features, and that is deliberate. This list carried 16 entries
# when the font was Maple Mono (cv03, cv05, cv09, cv10, cv61, cv38, cv42, cv43,
# ss03, ss07–ss11). Read straight out of Cascadia's GSUB table, its complete
# feature set is:
#   aalt calt case ccmp dnom fina frac init locl medi numr ordn
#   rclt rlig sinf ss02 ss19 ss20 subs sups zero
# So 14 of the 16 would have been silently ignored — a font feature that does
# not exist is not an error, it is simply absent. calt (ligatures) and zero
# (slashed zero) are the two that carry over. ss02/ss19/ss20 exist but carry no
# UI label in the font and Microsoft does not document them, so they are left
# off rather than cargo-culted.
#
# UPDATE: ss01 and ss03 turned out to exist after all — but ONLY in the italic
# faces. Read per-file straight out of each font's GSUB table. Re-verified
# 2026-09-04 against the files actually installed, whose names are Caskaydia,
# not Cascadia — the tags are unchanged, so nothing below needed revising:
#
#   CaskaydiaCoveNerdFont-Regular.ttf   ss02 ss19 ss20
#   CaskaydiaCoveNerdFont-Italic.ttf    ss01 ss02 ss03 ss19 ss20
#   (same split for every weight: roman gets 3, italic gets 5)
#
# That is why ss01 looked missing earlier — the Regular really does not have it.
# Listing it here is safe: a font that lacks a feature ignores it silently, so
# ss01 simply does nothing in roman text and activates in italics.
#
# ss19 is left off — it reportedly duplicates `zero`, which is already on.
# ss03 is available in the italics too if you want to experiment.
font-feature = calt, zero, ss01, ss02, ss20

# ── cursive italics ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cascadia Code's italic face IS the cursive one — it is a separate font file,
# not a stylistic set, which is why no `ss` toggle produces it. The Nerd Font
# patch preserves that, so CaskaydiaCoveNerdFont-Italic.ttf is still cursive.
#
# NB: both lines matter. Ghostty will happily SYNTHESISE an italic by shearing
# the roman face, and a synthetic slant is not cursive — it is the upright
# letterforms leaning over. `no-italic` in font-synthetic-style forbids that
# fallback, and font-style-italic names the real face. Without the pair you can
# get fake italics that look almost right and are not cursive at all.
font-style-italic = Italic
font-synthetic-style = bold,no-italic,bold-italic

# A little breathing room between lines; Caskaydia is tighter than Maple was.
adjust-cell-height = 8%

# ── theme ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Bundled with Ghostty, so no external file and no generator.
#
# NB: the value is the theme's FILE NAME, verbatim — "Catppuccin Latte", with a
# space and capitals. Not "catppuccin-latte". Ghostty looks for
# /usr/share/ghostty/themes/<value> and the shipped files are named exactly as
# `ghostty +list-themes` prints them (minus the trailing "(resources)").
#
# NB: `ghostty +show-config | grep theme` is NOT a check that this works. It
# echoes back whatever you wrote, because the value parsed fine as a string —
# the failure is at theme LOAD time, and it surfaces as a "Configuration Errors"
# dialog at startup, not in +show-config. The real check is whether a palette
# actually got loaded:
#     ghostty +show-config | grep -E '^(background|foreground) '
# Empty output means no theme was applied, whatever the theme line says.
theme = Catppuccin Latte

# ── how far the theme actually reaches ───────────────────────────────────────
# The theme file sets palette 0-15 plus background/foreground/cursor/selection.
# So programs emitting BASIC ANSI are recoloured to Catppuccin for free —
# verified: `eza` and `ls --color` emit only 30-37/90-97 and come out themed.
#
# Two things it does NOT reach, worth knowing before chasing a colour that
# refuses to change:
#   - palette 16-255, the 256-colour cube. Stock xterm (#ff0000, #00005f …),
#     which is outside the Catppuccin ramp. That is what palette-generate
#     below is for.
#   - 24-bit truecolor. `\e[38;2;R;G;B` names an exact colour and NO terminal
#     setting can remap it. Verified: bat, starship and
#     zsh-syntax-highlighting all emit truecolor — they look right only
#     because each is separately configured for Catppuccin Latte. Anything
#     else emitting truecolor (npm, dotnet, docker) keeps its own colours and
#     there is nothing to configure here about it.

# Derive palette 16-255 from the base 16 instead of using the stock xterm cube.
#
# NB — UNVERIFIED, deliberately left on with this warning rather than presented
# as working. `+show-config` still reports `palette = 196=#ff0000` with this
# enabled, which is either "generation happens at render time, after the config
# dump" or "it does nothing", and the CLI cannot tell those apart. This is the
# same trap as the top-of-file note: parsing is not resolving.
#
# Check it by eye, then keep or delete this line:
#   for i in $(seq 16 231); do printf "\033[48;5;${i}m  "; done; printf "\033[0m\n"
# Stock xterm gives you saturated primaries — pure #ff0000, #00ff00. Generated
# gives a muted ramp built out of the Latte accents.
#
# NB: Ghostty's docs warn that legacy programs hardcode the xterm cube, so if
# some tool starts rendering oddly in 256-colour mode, this is the first thing
# to turn off. `palette-harmonious` builds further on this and its own docs say
# it can make such programs unreadable — not enabled.
palette-generate = true

# A floor on text/background contrast, as a WCAG ratio.
#
# NB: 1.1, NOT the "3 or higher" the docs suggest for general readability. That
# advice assumes an arbitrary palette; this one is deliberately low-contrast.
# Measured against this theme's background (#eff1f5), 9 of the 16 palette
# colours fall below a ratio of 3.0 — br-white 1.61, br-yellow and white 1.91,
# yellow 2.31, magenta 2.34, br-green 2.48, br-cyan 2.80, green 2.96 — so
# `minimum-contrast = 3` would have Ghostty REWRITE nine of your sixteen
# colours toward black and the theme would be gone.
#
# At 1.1 nothing in the palette is touched (the lowest is 1.61), so this is a
# pure safety net: it only rescues output that is genuinely invisible, i.e.
# some tool printing near-white on near-white. That case is real on a light
# theme and costs nothing to guard against.
minimum-contrast = 1.1

# NB: window-theme must be told it is light. On "auto" Ghostty follows the
# desktop's colour-scheme preference, so a light terminal ends up wearing dark
# GTK/KDE decorations and the titlebar fights the content.
window-theme = light

# ── window ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Let KWin draw the frame so the terminal matches every other window.
#
# NB — this trades away the integrated tab bar, which is worth knowing before
# you conclude Ghostty has no tabs. It does: `ctrl+shift+t`, and the binds
# below. But WHERE they render depends on this setting:
#
#   window-decoration = server   KWin draws the titlebar, so Ghostty has no
#                                headerbar of its own to put tabs in. Tabs
#                                appear as a separate strip below the KDE
#                                titlebar. Matches every other KDE window.
#
#   window-decoration = client   Ghostty draws its own GTK/Adwaita headerbar
#                                with tabs integrated into it, GNOME-Console
#                                style. Looks better with many tabs, looks
#                                foreign next to Breeze windows.
#
# NB — and separately: the tab bar is hidden until there are TWO tabs, because
# `window-show-tab-bar` defaults to `auto`. That is not this setting's doing.
# Set `window-show-tab-bar = always` if you want it pinned open.
window-decoration = server
window-padding-x = 10
window-padding-y = 8
window-padding-balance = true

# Ported from the old Konsole profile, which is what actually got used day to
# day before Ghostty was themed.
window-width = 125
window-height = 30

# ── cursor ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
cursor-style = bar
cursor-style-blink = true

# ── cursor trail ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Ghostty 1.3.1 has no built-in cursor animation — `cursor-style-blink` is the
# whole of it. A trail is done with a custom shader, and Ghostty exposes
# uniforms specifically for this (iCurrentCursor, iPreviousCursor,
# iCurrentCursorColor, iTimeCursorChange). See shaders/cursor-trail.glsl.
#
# A tapered comet behind the cursor, cycling through the Catppuccin Latte accent
# ramp, on EVERY cursor move — typing, arrow keys and jumps alike. It REPLACES
# shaders/cursor-smear.glsl (deleted), which drew a uniform-width band.
#
# It takes Kitty's `cursor_trail` silhouette and two-speed decay but DEPARTS
# from it in the one place that matters: Kitty's `cursor_trail_start_threshold`
# is 2 cells precisely so that typing does not streak, and here it is 0. That
# is a deliberate choice, not an oversight — and it is the first thing to put
# back (START_THRESHOLD ≈ 1.0 in the shader) if the effect reads as too busy.
#
# NB: a one-cell keystroke only travels one cell, so the tail is anchored PAST
# where the cursor actually was to give it visible length. The trail therefore
# does not trace the cursor's real path, by design.
#
# NB: the palette is hardcoded from the Catppuccin Latte accents, matching
# starship.toml — NOT read from `iPalette[256]`, which is only the ANSI 16 plus
# the colour cube and is duller than the accent ramp. Ghostty is pinned to
# `theme = Catppuccin Latte` in the theme section ABOVE, so the two cannot drift
# apart — but change that theme and the trail's colours become wrong.
#
# NB: the path is relative to THIS file's directory.
# NB: a shader that fails to compile is SILENTLY IGNORED — no dialog, no error,
# nothing in +show-config. See the CORRECTION at the top of this file. Comment
# a line out to disable that one shader.
#
# NB — and this is the part that cost an afternoon: even a shader that loads and
# runs every frame can draw nothing a human can see. The first version of the
# trail did exactly that: with `cursor-style = bar` it derived its thickness
# from min(cursor width, height), which is the BAR's width, so it drew a
# ONE-PIXEL hairline for about six frames. Full write-up at the top of the
# shader. Compiling proves less than it looks like it proves.
#
# To compile-check a shader edit without launching anything — there is no
# glslangValidator on this machine, but libshaderc is:
#   1. prepend Ghostty's own GLSL prelude, which is embedded in the binary:
#        strings /usr/bin/ghostty | grep -B25 'void main() { mainImage'
#      NB: the binary ALSO carries a prose doc block describing these uniforms,
#      and the two DISAGREE — prose says `vec4 iCursorVisible`, the prelude says
#      `uniform int`. The prelude is the real one. GLSL lets you swizzle a
#      scalar, so following the prose compiles clean and misbehaves silently.
#   2. call shaderc_compile_into_spv on prelude+shader via python3 ctypes
#      against libshaderc_shared.so.1, target env opengl.
# Sanity-check any such harness with a deliberately broken shader first — an
# arrangement that reports SUCCESS for everything proves nothing.
#
# Trail SPEED and shape are not settings in this file — they are the constants
# at the top of the shader: START_THRESHOLD (Kitty's cursor_trail_start_
# threshold), DECAY_FAST / DECAY_SLOW (cursor_trail_decay), DECAY_RANGE,
# STRENGTH, FADE_START. Editing the shader needs no Ghostty restart;
# ctrl+shift+r reloads it.

# ORDER MATTERS. `custom-shader` may be repeated, and the shaders run as a
# CHAIN: each one receives the previous one's output as iChannel0, so this is a
# pipeline, not a set of independent layers.
#
#   1. cursor-row     a faint band on the cursor's row — "where is my prompt"
#                     after a long agent run. Runs first so the trail draws on
#                     top of it instead of being washed out by it.
#   2. cursor-trail   the Catppuccin Latte comet.
#
# NB: there was briefly a third, unfocused-dim.glsl, which dimmed the surface
# when `iFocus == 0`. DELETED, because Ghostty already does it: the built-in
# `unfocused-split-opacity` defaults to 0.7 and fades unfocused splits with no
# shader at all. Both fire on the same unfocused surface, so keeping the shader
# meant DOUBLE-dimming — and paying for an extra full-screen pass to get it.
# Between that built-in for splits and KWin's titlebar for whole windows, the
# case was already covered. Turn the dial below instead of writing a shader.
custom-shader = shaders/cursor-row.glsl
custom-shader = shaders/cursor-trail.glsl

# How much unfocused SPLITS fade. 1 disables the effect; the floor is 0.15.
# Ghostty's default is 0.7 — set explicitly here only so the mechanism is
# discoverable next to the shaders, since that is where it was looked for.
unfocused-split-opacity = 0.7

# ── scrollback ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NB: this is in BYTES, not lines, despite the name — and Ghostty's default is
# already a bounded 10000000 (10 MB), so writing that value would be a silent
# no-op that never appears in `+show-config`. Konsole's HistorySize=10000 was
# 10k LINES, roughly 1–2 MB at typical log widths, so 2 MB is the honest port
# and a real tightening. Ghostty 1.4 splits this into scrollback-limit-bytes /
# scrollback-limit-lines; switch to `scrollback-limit-lines = 10000` then.
# UPDATE: raised from 2 MB for agentic work. Claude Code and friends emit a lot
# of output, and being able to scroll back through a whole session is worth
# more here than the memory. 20 MB is roughly 100k lines at typical widths.
scrollback-limit = 20000000

# ── mouse and clipboard ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
mouse-hide-while-typing = true
# NB: only the two that are NOT already Ghostty's defaults. Nine other lines
# were removed from this file after checking each against
# `ghostty +show-config --default` — background-opacity, window-show-tab-bar,
# clipboard-write, clipboard-paste-protection, link-previews and friends all
# already ship with the value we wanted. Restating a default is not harmless
# here: it reads as a deliberate choice, so the next person changes the wrong
# line looking for an effect.
copy-on-select = clipboard
clipboard-read = allow

# ── shell ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
command = /bin/zsh
shell-integration = zsh
# cursor: shape follows vi mode. sudo: keeps the env through sudo.
# title: lets the shell set the window title.
shell-integration-features = cursor,sudo,title
confirm-close-surface = false

# ── tuned for long-running CLI agents ────────────────────────────────────────
# Scroll speed. Ghostty's default is `precision:1,discrete:3`; both tracks are
# deliberately BELOW it because smaller steps read as smoother. Raise them if
# you find yourself scrolling forever through long agent output — it is a
# straight trade between "feels smooth" and "covers ground".
#
#   discrete    lines per mouse-wheel detent. 3 (default) -> 2 -> now 1.
#   precision   a SEPARATE track for touchpads and hi-res wheels, which deliver
#               pixel-precise events. Left at 1 until now on the theory that
#               those are already smooth; halved because "already smooth" is
#               not the same as "not too fast", and the touchpad is what this
#               machine actually scrolls with.
#
# NB: fractional values are legal on the precision track — verified, since a
# rejected value here would be silently dropped rather than reported. The check
# has to isolate the config, because `+show-config` takes only --default,
# --changes-only and --docs: pass it any other flag and it prints NOTHING at
# all, which reads exactly like a rejected value. Do it this way instead:
#
#   mkdir -p /tmp/gc/ghostty && echo 'mouse-scroll-multiplier = precision:0.25,discrete:1' > /tmp/gc/ghostty/config
#   XDG_CONFIG_HOME=/tmp/gc ghostty +show-config | grep scroll-multiplier
#
# Echoed back = accepted. Absent = invalid (or identical to the default).
# Confirmed accepted: precision:0.5, 0.25, 0.1. Rejected: precision:bogus.
# Go to 0.25 if 0.5 is still faster than you want.
{{ if eq .profile "laptop" -}}
mouse-scroll-multiplier = precision:0.5,discrete:1
{{- else -}}
# discrete:3 on the desktop. The precision track is unchanged - it is the same
# hi-res-wheel path - but `discrete` is the notched-wheel one, and the 1 above
# was chosen against a touchpad, which never takes that branch at all. One line
# per detent on an actual mouse wheel is punishing; 3 is the conventional
# default that Ghostty's own `discrete:1` was lowered FROM.
mouse-scroll-multiplier = precision:0.5,discrete:3
{{- end }}

# NB: three settings were CHECKED and then deliberately NOT written here,
# because Ghostty's defaults already do the right thing and restating a default
# reads as a considered choice:
#   bell-features           already `no-system,no-audio,attention,title,no-border`
#                           — `attention` is the one that matters: when a long
#                           agent run rings the bell, the taskbar flags the
#                           window, so you can start something and walk away.
#   app-notifications       already `clipboard-copy,config-reload`
#   custom-shader-animation already `true`

# ── keybindings ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Konsole-shaped where it maps cleanly, so the muscle memory survives.
#
# NB — TWO THINGS TO KNOW BEFORE EDITING THIS BLOCK.
#
# 1. `ctrl+shift+left/right` below OVERRIDE Ghostty defaults, they do not add.
#    Ghostty 1.3.1 ships ctrl+shift+arrow_left/right as previous_tab/next_tab;
#    binding them to goto_split REPLACES that, so arrow tab-switching is gone
#    and tabs are reachable only via ctrl+tab / ctrl+shift+tab / ctrl+page_up /
#    ctrl+page_down. That is the Konsole shape and it is the intent — but it is
#    a trade, and Ghostty already ships ctrl+alt+arrow_* for goto_split if you
#    would rather have the defaults back. Verify any change with
#    `diff <(ghostty +list-keybinds) <(ghostty +list-keybinds --default)`.
#
# 2. Several lines here RESTATE Ghostty 1.3.1 defaults rather than change them:
#    ctrl+shift+t/n/c/v, ctrl+tab, ctrl+shift+tab, alt+one..four, ctrl+plus and
#    ctrl+zero all resolve to what Ghostty already does. That sits awkwardly
#    beside this file's own rule about not restating defaults, and it is kept
#    deliberately: this block is meant to read as the complete Konsole keymap in
#    one place. Do not extend the pattern to settings elsewhere in the file.
keybind = ctrl+shift+t=new_tab
keybind = ctrl+shift+n=new_window
keybind = ctrl+shift+w=close_surface
keybind = ctrl+shift+c=copy_to_clipboard
keybind = ctrl+shift+v=paste_from_clipboard
keybind = ctrl+shift+k=clear_screen
keybind = ctrl+shift+r=reload_config
keybind = f11=toggle_fullscreen

# splits
keybind = ctrl+shift+e=new_split:right
keybind = ctrl+shift+o=new_split:down
keybind = ctrl+shift+left=goto_split:left
keybind = ctrl+shift+right=goto_split:right
keybind = ctrl+shift+up=goto_split:up
keybind = ctrl+shift+down=goto_split:down
keybind = ctrl+shift+z=toggle_split_zoom
keybind = ctrl+shift+x=equalize_splits

# tabs
keybind = ctrl+tab=next_tab
keybind = ctrl+shift+tab=previous_tab
keybind = alt+one=goto_tab:1
keybind = alt+two=goto_tab:2
keybind = alt+three=goto_tab:3
keybind = alt+four=goto_tab:4

# font size
keybind = ctrl+plus=increase_font_size:1
keybind = ctrl+minus=decrease_font_size:1
keybind = ctrl+zero=reset_font_size
